# The facility.
#
# A neoclassical public building in the Ionic order: a hexastyle portico facing south on a
# podium reached by a broad flight of steps, a main block with a rusticated ground storey and a
# piano nobile above it, wings east and west, and a domed rotunda on the centre line.
#
# This file is a manifest and almost nothing else. The building is written in the fragments it
# includes, one to a file, because a building is made of parts and so should the thing that
# describes it be — and because that way many hands can work on it at once without any two of
# them editing the same lines.
#
# The order matters. `_contract.clip` first, because it holds every dimension the rest derives
# from and every material they are made of. `_order.clip` second, because it holds the Ionic
# vocabulary — a column, a pilaster, a capital, a baluster — that the parts place and repeat.
# Then the parts, outside to inside, and the paint stacks in the order they are included: a
# later coat covers an earlier one, so the base coat is here at the top and every fragment paints
# only where its own shapes are.
#
# See clips/facility/BRIEF.md for what each part is and the rules they are all written to.

include "facility/_contract.clip"
include "facility/_order.clip"

# The coat everything starts in. Limestone is what the building is, and every other material in
# it is an exception argued for by one of the fragments below.
paint limestone

# --- the ground it stands on ---------------------------------------------------------------
include "facility/site.clip"
include "facility/podium.clip"
include "facility/steps.clip"

# --- the shell -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
include "facility/walls.clip"
include "facility/pilasters.clip"
include "facility/windows.clip"
include "facility/doors.clip"
include "facility/portico.clip"
include "facility/entablature.clip"
include "facility/pediment.clip"
include "facility/parapet.clip"
include "facility/roof.clip"

# --- the dome ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
include "facility/drum.clip"
include "facility/dome.clip"

# --- what is inside it --------------------------------------------------------------------------
include "facility/vestibule.clip"
include "facility/rotunda.clip"
include "facility/halls.clip"
include "facility/stair.clip"

# --- and the state rooms, which are new -----------------------------------------------------
#
# Four corners of the main block had never been entered. The brief has always said the building
# has to be able to GROW -- "adding a room is one new file and three lines in the manifest" -- and
# these are those lines. They go here, after the four original rooms and before the fittings,
# because the fittings line below has to stay last among the inside parts for the reason its own
# comment gives.
#
# THE INTERIORS ARE NOT MIRRORED AND THE SHELL STILL IS. A salon east and a chapel west, a
# ballroom over one and a library over the other: mirroring them would mean two salons and no
# chapel. BRIEF.md rule 3 was amended in the same change to say the shell is symmetric and what
# is inside a room is not required to be.
include "facility/crypt.clip"
include "facility/salon.clip"
include "facility/chapel.clip"
include "facility/ballroom.clip"
include "facility/library.clip"
include "facility/enfilade.clip"
include "facility/terrace.clip"

include "facility/fittings.clip"

# --- and what the weather has done to all of it ------------------------------------------------
#
# Last, and that is the whole of why it is here rather than up with the site it mostly acts on.
# Weathering appends its coats after the author's, so whatever is included last is painted over
# everything before it — which is exactly right for sand, moss and stain, and exactly wrong if it
# is included in the middle.
#
# This line was missing for the building's whole first life. The fragment was written, the three
# `weather` statements in it were correct, and nothing included the file — so the one subsystem
# the facility exists to exercise was silently absent, and the only sign of it was that the report
# said one weathering coat where there should have been thirteen.
include "facility/surface.clip"

# --- the rest of the estate ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The facility is not one building. Everything above this line is the neoclassical block at the
# centre; everything below it is another building on the same ground, each authored as its own
# standalone clip in its own coordinates and carried here by the one translate named in its file.
# The vectors, and the three that had to change for collisions, are the table in _contract.clip.
#
# INCLUDED AFTER surface.clip ON PURPOSE, and the reason is that this changes nothing above it.
# The paint stacks in include order, so anything inserted between two of the building's own
# fragments moves the building's colours. These are appended instead: every rule in surface.clip
# is scoped (17 `paint ... where=`, 2 `weather ... on=`) and every one of those scopes is a box
# inside the block's own footprint, x +/-16.875, z -16.5 .. 8.55 -- so the building's weathering
# cannot reach out here, and these fragments' own coats cannot reach back.
include "facility/campanile.clip"
include "facility/grotto.clip"
include "facility/theatre.clip"
include "facility/pavilion.clip"

# --- assembly ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

let shell = union {
    part_site part_podium part_steps
    part_walls part_pilasters part_windows part_doors
    part_portico part_entablature part_pediment part_parapet part_roof
    part_drum part_dome
}

let inside = union {
    part_vestibule part_rotunda part_halls part_stair part_surface
    part_crypt part_salon part_chapel part_ballroom part_library part_enfilade
}

let built = union { shell inside }

# The voids come last, so nothing put in afterwards can be eaten by a room being carved. The
# fragments that need to remove matter — a doorway through a wall, a window through it, the
# rooms themselves — each expose one shape named `<part>_void`, and they are all taken away
# here, in one place, where the order is visible.
#
# THIRTY-ONE PARTS AND TWELVE VOIDS, AND EVERY ONE OF EACH IS ACCOUNTED FOR. Fourteen parts in
# `shell`, eleven in `inside`, part_fittings and part_terrace joined below, and FOUR in
# `grounds`: thirty-one, which is every `part_` bound in clips/facility/, each named exactly once.
# No grounds part exposes a void -- grotto.clip says so in as many words and the other three take
# nothing from anybody either, so the manifest's twelve voids are still twelve. Twelve `void_` are bound and
# twelve are subtracted here, so there is no void without a part and no part whose void is left
# out. The three parts that expose no void say so in their own files, in as many words:
# surface.clip ("there is no void_surface"), terrace.clip ("it removes nothing from anybody") and
# fittings.clip.
#
# WHAT EACH VOID ACTUALLY REMOVES, and this is measured rather than reasoned. Each row is the
# building sampled twice at metre 8 — once as it ships, once with that one void left out of this
# difference — and the difference between the two volumes. `hollowed` is 1,860,941 voxels, 179
# material records, 719 components.
#
#     void            removed by it alone     what it is
#     void_crypt              281,083         the basement excavated out of the podium
#     void_windows             54,809         every window opening   ** and 276 components **
#     void_doors                9,422         the great door and the two side doors
#     void_halls                6,436         the two arches into the rotunda
#     void_vestibule            5,022         the vestibule's air, less its own stone
#     void_rotunda              3,400         the coffers, and the oculus that must stay open
#     void_library                145         a cornice of somebody else's left in the room
#     void_enfilade                78         the five niches, where they reach the halls' block
#     void_stair                        0     a buried point; 8 voxels at metre 16, invisible
#     void_salon                        0     4,600 voxels at metre 32, ALL of it also void_doors
#     void_chapel                      -6     nothing
#     void_ballroom                   -54     nothing
#
# THE MATERIAL COUNT IS 179 ON EVERY ONE OF THE THIRTEEN ROWS. No void in this building removes
# the last voxel of any material.
#
# THE NEGATIVE NUMBERS ARE NOT MATTER MOVING. A set difference cannot add matter, and `despeckle`
# states in its own header that it changes no geometry, so -6 and -54 on 1.86 million voxels are
# the sampler settling blocks differently for a CSG tree with one operand fewer. They are read as
# zero, and the reading is confirmed the other way round: `intersection { built void_chapel }` and
# `intersection { built void_ballroom }` are both EMPTY at metre 16 AND at metre 32.
#
# A VOID THAT REMOVES NOTHING IS NOT AUTOMATICALLY A BUG, and steps.clip is the reason that sentence
# is here rather than an alarm: it had a cut that removed nothing because `offset ... by=-0.045`
# grew the shape instead of shrinking it, and it sampled to the identical voxel count on both sides.
# The four quiet voids here are the other thing. void_chapel, void_ballroom and void_salon are
# `difference { <room>_air part_<room> }` over rooms that carve their own air inside their own mass,
# so there is nothing of anybody else's left in them TO remove — which is what a correct room void
# looks like when every neighbour has behaved. They are the guard, not the excavation, and the day a
# fragment leaves a wall in the ballroom they will start removing it. Do not delete them for being
# quiet; check them by putting something in the room and watching it go.
let hollowed = difference {
    built
    void_doors void_windows void_vestibule void_rotunda void_halls void_stair
    void_crypt void_salon void_chapel void_ballroom void_library void_enfilade
}

# AND THE FURNITURE AFTER THE ROOMS, which is what the paragraph above always claimed happened.
#
# It did not. `part_fittings` was in `inside`, `inside` is in `built`, and every room's void is
# subtracted from `built` — so every sconce, bench and statue standing in a room was cut away by
# the room it stands in. A room's void is correctly written as *the air of that room less that
# room's own stone*, which is what stops another fragment leaving a wall in somebody's room; but a
# fitting is BY DEFINITION a thing standing in somebody's air, and it went with it.
#
# What survived was the 0.09 m of backplate buried in the wall: a flat dark rectangle with no
# bracket, no bowl and no flame. Reported from playing as *"i cant tell what they are"*, which is
# the exact right reaction to the back of a lamp. The benches, buried the same 0.09 and projecting
# 0.45 into the room, went altogether, and so did both hall statues.
#
# It also took the light with it. halls.clip builds two rooms with no window in either and says in
# its own header that everything past the first bay arrives from a sconce or from nothing; the
# eight sconces are that "or from nothing", and with their bowls and flames cut off the halls were
# lit by nothing at all. The whole emissive load of the building indoors was one deleted union.
#
# fittings.clip diagnosed this from inside its own file, measured it, wrote the fix out in
# clips/facility/requests/fittings.md and could not make it, because the line is here. This is that
# line. It cannot open a seam: part_fittings removes no matter, exposes no void of its own, and
# overlaps its hosts by 0.05 everywhere. The grain still runs over it, because `all` displaces what
# comes out of here and not what went in.
# A DOOR AND A WINDOW STILL CUT A FITTING. A ROOM DOES NOT. That is the whole of the distinction
# this line exists to make, and the first version of it did not make it: `union { hollowed
# part_fittings }` put the furniture back after EVERY void, so a sconce standing in a doorway was
# no longer cut by the doorway. Reported from playing, immediately, as a lamp on one of the lesser
# doors -- a bronze bracket with its bowl alight, hanging in mid-air in front of a closed door
# leaf, which is what the portico's outer sconce has always been and what `void_doors` had always
# quietly been hiding.
#
# The two kinds of void are not the same kind of thing and it took a wrong picture to see it:
#
#   THE AIR OF A ROOM. A fitting stands in a room's air by definition, and subtracting these
#   deleted every sconce, bench and statue indoors (D608). They must not touch part_fittings:
#
#       void_vestibule  void_rotunda  void_halls  void_stair
#       void_crypt  void_salon  void_chapel  void_ballroom  void_library  void_enfilade
#
#   AN OPENING THROUGH A WALL. Nothing may stand in one -- not a fitting, not anything -- because
#   the hole is the point. These must still cut part_fittings, exactly as they did before D608:
#
#       void_doors  void_windows
#
# THE SIX ROOMS ON THE FIRST LIST WERE ADDED AFTER THAT RULE WAS WRITTEN, and the whole point of
# an audit is that being in the right list is not the same as being written in the right list.
# Every one of the six is `difference { <room>_air part_<room> }`, which is the room-air form, and
# every one is subtracted from `built` alone. Checked by measurement rather than by reading, at
# metre 32, as `intersection { part_fittings void_<room> }`:
#
#     void_salon  void_chapel  void_ballroom  void_library  void_enfilade   EMPTY
#     void_crypt                                                           1,508 voxels
#
# THE CRYPT ONE IS THE REASON TO DO THIS BY MEASUREMENT. Nothing about a crypt two storeys down
# suggests it can reach a statue standing outdoors under the portico, and it does: `void_crypt`
# runs up to y 1.844 beneath the portico floor, and fittings.clip beds the foot of its plinths and
# torchere pedestals to y 1.750 so they cannot float. 0.094 m of overlap, x +-3.60 .. 4.50 and
# +-1.875 .. 2.625, z -8.312 .. -8.000. On the wrong list that void saws the feet off four pieces
# of furniture in the one room everybody looks at first.
#
# FIXED IN fittings.clip, NOT LEFT: the outer portico sconce was at x = +-5.40 and the side door's
# void is x = 4.50 .. 6.30, so it stood squarely in a doorway. The note that used to sit here said
# it "comes out cut in half". It did not. Measured as `intersection { part_fittings void_doors }`
# at metre 32: 1,768 voxels in two components, and a whole sconce is 884 voxels -- so BOTH copies
# went entirely, and the portico's four sconces had been two for the whole of this building's life.
# fittings.clip now places it at x = +-3.15, which is the only plain wall this elevation has
# (doors_gd_case ends at 2.031, doors_sd_case begins at 3.96, the statue's plinth holds
# 3.594 .. 4.500), and the same intersection is EMPTY.
# THREE THINGS THIS AUDIT FOUND THAT THIS FILE CANNOT FIX, written down here because this is the
# file that would have to know about them and none of the three has any symptom in the report.
#
# 1. `part_surface` IS ENTIRELY EATEN BY `void_crypt`, AND IT IS THE ONLY GEOMETRY surface.clip HAS.
#    Its setting-out stone is a 0.225 cube at (0, 0.675, -2.25) which its own file calls "buried in
#    the middle of the podium's mass, granite on all six sides of it". The crypt was excavated out
#    of that podium afterwards. Measured at metre 32: `surface_datum` is 420 voxels,
#    `intersection { surface_datum void_crypt }` is 420 voxels — all of it — and
#    `intersection { hollowed surface_datum }` is EMPTY. So is a probe of the 0.30 box around it:
#    there is not even neighbouring stone, the datum stands in open crypt air.
#    THE CONSEQUENCE IS A PAINT RULE THAT CANNOT FIRE. surface.clip's `paint granite
#    where=surface_datum below=0.02` has no voxel to land on. That is the failure clipcheck's
#    "never fired" line exists to catch, arriving from a direction nobody was watching: not a rule
#    written wrong, a rule whose stone another fragment carried away two storeys down.
#    IT IS NOT FIXABLE HERE. Moving `part_surface` out of `inside` so the voids miss it would leave
#    a 0.225 granite cube floating in the crypt's air — a mystery block in a room, and one more
#    component. The datum has to move, and only surface.clip can move it.
#
# 2. `void_rotunda` IS BOTH KINDS OF VOID IN ONE BINDING. It is `union { rotunda_coffers
#    rotunda_oculus }`. The coffers are recesses in a ceiling and belong on the room list, exactly
#    where they are. The oculus does not: rotunda.clip's own words for it are "taken away by the
#    manifest after everything in the building is assembled, so that nothing added to this scene
#    later can close the one aperture the whole interior is lit through" — which IS the door and
#    window argument, word for word. On today's geometry it makes no difference:
#    `intersection { part_fittings void_rotunda }` is EMPTY at metre 32, because the corona's four
#    rods stop at y 17.70 on a 1.80 radius and the coffering begins outside them. Splitting it needs
#    rotunda.clip to expose the oculus as a void of its own; naming `rotunda_oculus` from here would
#    make one fragment's private binding part of the manifest's contract, which BRIEF.md rule 2
#    forbids. Left as it is, deliberately, and written down so the next hanging light is checked.
#
# 3. `void_windows` IS THE ONLY VOID IN THE BUILDING THAT SHATTERS MATTER. Every other void leaves
#    the component count at 719; without void_windows the building is 443 components, so it is
#    making 276 of them on its own. That is `clips/facility/requests/walls-thickness.md` seen from
#    the assembly end — a cutter punching 1.80 m through a 0.90 m wall carries 0.90 m on into the
#    room and slices whatever is standing there into islands. windows.clip owns the cutter.
#
# AND THE ROOF GARDEN WITH THE FURNITURE, for the same reason and not quite the same one.
#
# `part_terrace` is pots, seats, lanterns, an armillary sphere and a cistern standing on the lead
# deck. Like a fitting it stands in somebody's air and must not be cut by a room; UNLIKE a fitting
# it is not in a doorway and never can be, because every door and window in this building is at
# least two and a half metres below the deck it stands on. So it joins here rather than in
# `inside`, and it does NOT go through the door-and-window difference: passing it through one
# would be a subtraction that can only ever remove something by accident.
#
# CHECKED, ALL TWELVE, at metre 32: `intersection { part_terrace void_<any> }` is EMPTY for every
# void in the building. So both halves of the paragraph above are true and neither is load-bearing
# today — the terrace would survive being put anywhere in this assembly. It stays here because the
# argument is about what a future void may reach, not about what today's do, and the crypt is the
# standing proof that a void reaches further than its name suggests.
# The other buildings of the estate, joined for the same reason part_terrace is joined here and
# not in `inside`: they stand outside the block, no void in this manifest is meant to reach them,
# and passing them through the door-and-window difference could only ever remove something by
# accident. Each brings its own ground and its own weathering and takes nothing away from anybody.
let grounds = union { part_campanile part_grotto part_theatre part_pavilion }

let furnished = union { hollowed  difference { part_fittings void_doors void_windows }  part_terrace  grounds }

# --- surface --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Twelve millimetres of grain over everything — AND THE SAMPLER DROPS IT, AT EVERY RESOLUTION THIS
# GAME IS EVER BUILT AT. The line stays; the comment that used to sit here does not, because it was
# wrong and it was the load-bearing sentence in two other documents.
#
# It read: "under half a voxel at full detail, so it moves no wall — and is the difference between
# a face that reads as carved stone and one that reads as a drawing of carved stone." Both halves
# cannot be true at once, and `usable_displacement` in src/forge/clip_script.cpp quotes this exact
# line back and says why: being under a voxel is not what makes a displacement harmless, it is
# precisely what makes it DITHER, because a displacement too small to move a wall is still large
# enough to flip the voxels the wall is made of. On the flat axis-aligned faces this building is
# mostly made of, the whole face dithers at once — a raspy speckle that became black pepper once
# the renderer had real sun and sky, because a flipped voxel exposes a face pointing a different
# way and a differently-facing face is several times darker.
#
# So anything under HALF A VOXEL is dropped rather than scaled up, and 0.012 m is 0.38 of a voxel
# at metre 32 and 0.10 at metre 8. There is no resolution this building is sampled at where this
# line does anything at all, and the log has said so in two WARN lines on every single build:
#
#     displace amount 0.012 m is 0.38 of a voxel at metre 32 -- ... Dropped.
#     weather  amount 0.0045 m is 0.14 of a voxel at metre 32 -- ... Dropped.
#
# THE BEHAVIOUR IS CORRECT AND IS NOT BEING CHANGED HERE. Raising the amount to 0.016 would make it
# apply and would bring back the pepper; the honest answer at this grid is the undisplaced surface,
# which is what the sampler already gives. What was wrong was a comment claiming a visual effect
# the build has not produced for as long as the number has read 0.012 — and BRIEF.md rule 5, whose
# whole justification for `below=0.02` was this grain pushing voxels outside their own shape. It
# does not. The rind that rule prevents is real, but it comes from coverage-versus-centre on
# transformed geometry, which is why that rule now names 0.035 as well.
let all = displace { furnished grain_fine } amount=0.012


variation colour=0.030 rough=0.070 seed=17

solid all

# --- where the origin is ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The player spawns at 0,0,0 and should be standing in the middle of the rotunda floor looking out
# of the main door, so the BUILDING moves to meet that rather than the spawn moving to meet the
# building. The origin is the one coordinate everybody types; it should mean something.
#
# The rotunda floor is a cylinder centred at y 1.7325 and 0.135 thick, so its top face is at 1.80.
# The origin wants to be an eye height above it: 1.80 + 1.70 = 3.50 m down. Its centre is already at
# x 0, z 0 — the plan was drawn around it — so nothing moves sideways, and the main door is the
# opening on the minus-z face, which is the way the spawn looks.
#
# `origin` and not a translate around the solid. A translate moves the stone and leaves every paint
# rule at the height it was drawn for, which put the plinth course and the ground line partway up
# the walls — geometry right, colour wrong. This moves both.
#
# AND IT SILENTLY BREAKS EVERY `--part` MEASUREMENT TAKEN ON THIS FILE, which cost most of a session
# to notice. `apply_origin` translates the node bound to `solid` and every paint rule, and shifts the
# clip's bounds to match — it does NOT translate the other 2,500 named bindings, because nothing
# reaches them. `clipcheck --part <name>` then swaps an UNTRANSLATED node into `script.solid` while
# the bounds have already moved 3.50 m down, so a part is sampled in model space against a box that
# has been shifted out from under it. Everything above model y 16.50 falls outside the box and is
# reported as if it were not there: `--part dome_lan_ball` on the gilt ball at 19.51 says EMPTY, and
# `--part part_fittings` reports the corona's hanging rods stopping dead at 16.500.
#
# THE WHOLE BUILDING IS FINE — `all` is the translated node, so the shipped sample is right, and this
# is a measuring fault and not a building one. But any `--part` figure taken from a clip with an
# `origin` is only trustworthy below `bounds.high.y + shift`. Comment this line out while probing.
origin 0 -3.50 0
